A||||14||Ozzy||2.5L Turbo Chargers.   Where???
Z||000000||Ozzy||08-15-99||07:48 PM||||Although some may think I'm out of my mind for considering such a thing, I still keep coming up blank trying to find someone who produces a turbo system for the 4cyl. I have heard the names Garret and IRM and also read in a thread somewhere that an old article in Hot Rod magazine described a Chrysler turbo being modified fairly easily for the 'Duke' in an'84 by Smokey Yunick without major mods to the engine. <p>Personally I hear anywhere from $3000.00 to $8,000.00 for a V6 transplant and then problems. My engine has been modified, rebuilt very recently with new clutch, yada yada yada. It supposedly produces 135hp on the dyno. Now if I can put a turbo on for around $1,800.00 to $2,000.00 and gain maybe 70 horses then seems to me I might be in better shape with an overall vehichle weight slightly less than the V6.<p>Just a thought anyway...this seems to be a contraversial subject on here and I am by no means an expert therefore appreciate continued input.<p>Ozzy
Z||000001||Cooter||08-15-99||09:53 PM||||I used to have high hopes of making my iron duck into a hot rod, but I have just accepted the little beast as it is. I know that a turbo and all the stuff would make more power, but when i want more power I just hop into the Suburban (fuel injected 454-400+++ horses and 500 ft/lb of torque) The fiero is a great daily driver and I am not going to get into anything that would adversly affect driveability and economy. I hope to get an 88 formula soon- then we can talk modifications. Just enjoy what you have and be greatful that someone has done the hard stuff for you.
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||08-15-99||10:07 PM||||My Fiero had the 2.5 in it but now has the 2.8 V6 in it. There is a very small amount of room left for the turbo I am installing. You should have tons of room to install one.<p>Are you wanting to buy a kit or build one? If you built your own it would be pennys compared to a kit. Design One makes a turbo kit for the 2.8 V6 and costs $4,000. I know of a guy that put a turbo on his 87GT auto for under $800. It may not look pretty but his wallet still does. <p>If you do plan to build your own(I am not aware of a kit being offered for the 2.5) I think it would be easy to find a turbo. Chrystler and Ford used them in the mid 80s in their cars. I got one from an 87 T-Bird and cost me 50 bucks. I plan to rebuild my turbo with a $75 kit. The place I got mine from has about 10 cars with turbos in them at the yard. Only 1 turbo remains though. 
Z||000003||BN Boomer||08-16-99||07:22 AM||||I want to know how Cooter is getting 400+++ HP and 500 ft/pds of torque out of his Suburban's 454. My 94 K2500 Suburban's TBI 454 is factory rated at a measly 230 HP but 385 ft/pds peak torque @ 1800rpm. With 4.10 gears and the Gov-Loc it has mucho grunt, but I sure wouldn't mind a little, okay A LOT, more top end performance out of it.  
Z||000004||BN Boomer||08-16-99||07:23 AM||||I want to know how Cooter is getting 400+++ HP and 500 ft/pds of torque out of his Suburban's 454. My 94 K2500 Suburban's TBI 454 is factory rated at a measly 230 HP but 385 ft/pds peak torque @ 1800rpm. With 4.10 gears and the Gov-Loc it has mucho grunt, but I sure wouldn't mind a little, okay A LOT, more top end performance out of it.  
Z||000005||four50four||08-16-99||03:36 PM||||me too, i want to know where that power is coming from. i know its possible but with fuel injection thats gettin' kind of expensive.
Z||000006||Ozzy||08-16-99||04:36 PM||||Not Me... I only want to know about how to get mo' power out o' ma FIERO!!! I go to the "Suburban Forum" to find out stuff like that! (toungue firmly wedged in cheek. ;-))<p>SCCA Fiero...Do you know of a suitable turbo to start with?? I like the idea of building one myself but feel pretty ignorant toward the subject as well as the operating parameters of the 2.5L engine. Hell... the way you describe it, if an installation for 100's of dollars as compared to 1,000's, even if it only produced a 30 to 40 hp gain I see that as a 'value for money' upgrade. (are my figures unreasonable??) I write off my time as all of this is still a learning /hobby type experience so it's really wallet impact that counts.<p>I still hope to gain more pro's and cons from you all to help in my decision here.<p>thnx <p>Ozzy
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||08-16-99||05:53 PM||||I would think the same turbo I have would be fine. You can set the wastegate to 5psi to be safe to start with. The motor my turbo came from was a 2.3 Ford engine. Look in the bonyards for 85-88 T-birds and about the same year Lebaron. I would try to find one with water coolant lines in them (I think 87-88 t-birds are water cooled and have the intercooler). Expect to pay from 50-100 bucks for the turbo and not any more than 125. I wouldn't pay more than that anyway.
Z||000008||Cooter||08-16-99||06:51 PM||||400 hp and 500 ft/lb= 1974 big block 454 , steel crank, Edelbrock aluminum rpm heads with BIG valves, "decent" roller cam advanced 4 degrees for low end torque, 1.8 ratio true roller rockers, .030 over 11 to 1 forged pistons, fully balanced rotating asembly, stepped tube headers into 2.5 duals with crossover and flowmasters, Holley Projection 4di 900 cfm throttle body fuel injection on Holley intake. The power/ torque figures are conservative and yes the 6000 pound 3/4 ton 'Burb will tow pretty much anything I hook to it. It is also a lot of FUN to drive, just gets really bad gas mileage, I have no idea why. . .
Z||000009||Cooter||08-16-99||07:16 PM||||Oh, one more thing- I did this engine build before I got married and had more money than sense! Just in case you wonder what elementary school teachers do during the summer, they build truck motors. If the wife knew how much cash was tied up in the "bus" (that what she calls it), I would probably be in the need for a new wife. But then again, if she left I could spend all my money on projects again. A 454 powered Fiero???? I wonder-
Z||000010||william_thorniii||08-16-99||10:51 PM||||The specific turbocharger that everyone is referring to is the Garret T-3. This is the turbo utilized on all Ford Thunderbird Supercoupes with the 2.3L and the Chrysler 2.2L engines.  Pulling the parts is the easiest part of the job.  Most every junkyard that has mid-eighties Chryslers and Fords will have at least one.  Make sure that you visit Dennis LaGrua's webpage to find out how he fitted a T-3 to his 2.8L Fiero.  The hardest part is getting the computer and associated electronics to work out properly.  Other than that, take time finding a good exhaust bender/welder as you will need custom exhaust work to fabricate the specific intake/exhaust needed to plumb the turbo.  Other than that, let me know how it works!!!  I have been wanting to do this for a while now.  
Z||000011||terryk||08-17-99||12:19 AM||||Just stick an Olds 804 in there and be done with it (since we are escalating this motor size rapidly).<p>Screw it, an Allison.<p>On the more serious side, a friend and I are installing a turbo on a V6. We are using a 280Z turbo and a 1227749 ECM from Sunbird GT using the Syclone/Typhoon code. Email me privately if you want an update.
